WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.220
Hi everyone, and welcome to Biology Professor.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.910
Today, we're talking about photosynthesis
and cell respiration.

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:17.570
Basically, we're going to look at how those
two are connected and what they have in common.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:21.430
Now, they're actually a lot more connected
than most people think.

00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:29.080
They're part of a large cycle that starts
with energy that comes from the sun.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:45.110
The sun provides light energy that is absorbed
by chloroplasts.

00:00:45.110 --> 00:00:48.370
This is where photosynthesis happens.

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:52.370
Photosynthesis yields two types of products.

00:00:52.370 --> 00:00:54.590
First, organic molecules.

00:00:54.590 --> 00:01:05.500
Most common one you will hear about is glucose,
so a type of sugar.

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:13.220
The other major product yielded by photosynthesis
is oxygen or O2.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:20.090
Organic molecules, like glucose, and oxygen
are then used by mitochondria in cellular

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:28.350
respiration.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:35.560
This is where the mitochondria have enzymes
present that, through several complicated

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:42.829
steps, break down organic molecules using
oxygen as a final electron acceptor to generate

00:01:42.829 --> 00:01:46.480
something very, very important - ATP.

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:56.119
ATP is basically the energy currency of the
cell.

00:01:56.119 --> 00:01:57.310
It's like a dollar bill.

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:58.870
Everywhere in the cell takes it.

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:05.340
And this is where the energy is stored that
the cell can later use for a variety of cellular

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:06.340
processes.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:09.890
Now, where does this cycle part come in.

00:02:09.890 --> 00:02:15.140
In mitochondria, with cell respiration, once
they have used the organic molecules and the

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:19.140
oxygen, there are two different products that
are formed.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:29.910
These are carbon dioxide and water.

00:02:29.910 --> 00:02:37.410
Carbon dioxide and water are the two reactants
that chloroplasts need to carry out photosynthesis,

00:02:37.410 --> 00:02:40.260
so that is how this is one big cycle.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:47.300
Now, let's look at what these processes, photosynthesis
and cell respiration, what do they have in

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:48.300
common?

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:58.570
Well, they both use electron transport chains.

00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:05.519
Electron transport chains are these series
of enzymes that are part of biological membranes

00:03:05.519 --> 00:03:13.690
that pass electrons from place to place within
that chain, and in so doing, they harvest

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:18.690
energy from those electrons and create something
called a proton gradient.

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:30.000
The proton gradient is then used in both photosynthesis
and cell respiration by ATP synthase.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.299
And this is another common misconception.

00:03:32.299 --> 00:03:37.319
People think that ATP is only involved in
cell respiration, and it's true that cell

00:03:37.319 --> 00:03:44.810
respiration is where the cell gets the ATP
that it needs for all of its different functions,

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:48.950
but photosynthesis has ATP synthase as well.

00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:54.980
In photosynthesis, ATP synthase is used to
generate ATP in an early step of photosynthesis

00:03:54.980 --> 00:04:02.920
that is then used up in a later step of photosynthesis
where the organic molecules are being made,

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.450
so ATP is a part of both of these things.

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:17.299
Also, both photosynthesis and cell respiration
in plants, algae, and photosynthetic bacteria.

00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:25.220
People are tempted to say that autotrophs
like plants use photosynthesis and heterotrophs

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.530
like animals use cell respiration.

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:35.460
That's true, but actually, plants use BOTH,
and algae and photosynthetic bacteria, they

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:36.460
use both.

00:04:36.460 --> 00:04:42.221
They use the photosynthesis to generate the
organic molecules and the oxygen and they

00:04:42.221 --> 00:04:47.580
use the mitochondria that they have for cell
respiration to get the ATP that they need

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:49.680
for all of their various functions.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:55.020
Now, I also want to mention as a side note
that photosynthetic bacteria - you might be

00:04:55.020 --> 00:05:00.840
saying "well, how can they have photosynthesis,
when they don't have chloroplasts?"

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.090
That's true.

00:05:03.090 --> 00:05:06.320
Bacteria are prokaryotic cells, so they lack
organelles.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:10.210
So they don't have chloroplasts, they don't
have mitochondria.

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:15.840
Instead, the enzymes that are used in photosynthesis
and cell respiration, they have as part of

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:23.320
their plasma membrane, instead of being specifically
in the cells in these compartmentalized places.

00:05:23.320 --> 00:05:28.940
Now, another thing that these two processes
have in common is that they use biological

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:32.600
membranes for increased surface area.

00:05:32.600 --> 00:05:40.120
What does this mean?

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:47.740
Well, cells are very small places and they
have a lot going on inside of them.

00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:55.060
You see that chloroplasts have this double
membrane system and then on the inside, they've

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:56.229
got more membranes.

00:05:56.229 --> 00:05:59.350
The membranes here are in black.

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:01.759
And the mitochondrion also is a double membrane.

00:06:01.759 --> 00:06:06.960
You've got the outer membrane and then you've
got this inner membrane that has lots and

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.259
lots of folds.

00:06:08.259 --> 00:06:12.610
These folds are called cristae.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:22.270
In chloroplasts, all of these individual little
ovals are called thylakoids.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:33.460
And the thylakoids, when stacked together
in a column like this, form what's called

00:06:33.460 --> 00:06:35.440
a granum.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:46.021
So regardless of the terminology, in both
cases, you have a lot of biological membrane

00:06:46.021 --> 00:06:53.870
that is folded up into a very little space
and it's these biological membranes - the

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:58.259
thylakoid membranes and the cristae membranes
- that hold those electron transport chains

00:06:58.259 --> 00:07:04.310
where a big portion of photosynthesis and
cell respiration is happening.

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:09.770
So by having all of these folds or all of
these stacks of thylakoids, this provides

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:17.240
a lot of increased surface area so that photosynthesis
and cell respiration can happen at a very

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:18.240
high rate.

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:23.040
There's a lot of it going on at once because
it has all of this surface area to work with

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.250
even though it's in a small compartment.

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:31.610
And finally, another place that photosynthesis
and cell respiration are linked is in the

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:33.539
endosymbiotic theory.

00:07:33.539 --> 00:07:40.440
This theory deals with the evolution of eukaryotes.

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:52.070
So, eukaryotes (as different from prokaryotes),
eukaryotes are these cells that have a lot

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:59.560
of membrane-bound organelles, so organelles
like chloroplasts, like mitochondria, but

00:07:59.560 --> 00:08:04.870
also other organelles as well, lysosomes,
endoplasmic reticulum, the golgi apparatus,

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:11.110
the nucleus, all of these are specific to
eukaryotes.

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:19.349
The endosymbiotic theory says that mitochondria
and chloroplasts are special though, that

00:08:19.349 --> 00:08:26.880
they were formed when an early ancestor of
eukaryotic cells engulfed a photosynthetic

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:35.270
prokaryotes that then became a chloroplast
and a non-photosynthetic eukaryote that then

00:08:35.270 --> 00:08:45.660
became a mitochondrion, and that these two
organelles come from this ancient symbiosis

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:51.060
that was formed when prokaryotes were engulfed
by an early eukaryote.

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:56.339
So that is one other thing that photosynthesis
and cell respiration have in common.

00:08:56.339 --> 00:08:57.940
That is it for today.

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:00.200
I hope you enjoyed it and thanks for watching!

